Source package layout
=====================

This source package has two upstream tarballs:

  intel-crashlog_<version>.orig.tar.xz         the sources exported from Git
  intel-crashlog_<version>.orig-vendor.tar.xz  the Rust dependencies

The crates this project depends on are bundled rather than taken from the
librust-*-dev packages in the archive: dpkg-source unpacks the second tarball
into vendor/, debian/rules points Cargo at it through a source replacement
stanza in $CARGO_HOME, and the build runs with CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true and
--locked. The build therefore never reaches the network, and never re-resolves
the dependency tree.

Most of the dependencies of the library and of the command line tool do exist in
the archive, so those two could be built against them; the crates the UEFI
application needs, the uefi family and its dependencies, do not, and neither
does a Rust standard library for the x86_64-unknown-uefi target. Bundling keeps
the three crates building the same way and keeps the build reproducible against
the lock files. It is also what Ubuntu asks for: the Main Inclusion Review rules
for Rust require packages in main to vendor their Rust dependencies rather than
rely on the individual crate packages. What is bundled is declared in
debian/copyright and in the XS-Vendored-Sources-Rust field of debian/control, and
Static-Built-Using records the one thing that does come from another source
package: the Rust standard library, linked statically in from libstd-rust-dev.

XS-Vendored-Sources-Rust lists every bundled crate as CRATE@VERSION. It is the
union of the three lock files, since the three crates share one vendored tree,
and it is checked during dh_auto_configure by dh-cargo-vendored-sources, which
prints the expected value when the field and vendor/ have drifted apart.
`debian/rules vendored-sources` prints the same list on demand. The script is
currently only shipped by the Ubuntu build of dh-cargo, so the check is skipped
where it is absent; the field is still expected to be correct there.

Two things the Ubuntu rules for Rust in main require are satisfied without any
work here, and are worth re-checking when the dependency tree changes. Nothing
in the tree does TLS, so the requirement to use the system OpenSSL rather than
rustls does not apply. And although the UEFI crate has a rust-toolchain.toml,
it only requests the x86_64-unknown-uefi target and pins neither a channel nor a
compiler version, so it does not fall under the rule that crates pinning a
toolchain are kept out of the archive; a stable rustc builds it, and rustup is
needed only for the standard library of that target.

cargo vendor cannot restrict itself to the platforms that are actually built, so
the tree also holds the Windows halves of jiff and of the clap colour support,
which are a large part of it. Ubuntu suggests stripping those out;
packaging/vendor.sh --prune-windows does it, but that is not the default, since a
crate cargo still resolves and can no longer find is a build failure that nothing
in this repository would catch. Turning it on means confirming an offline
--locked build of all three crates and regenerating XS-Vendored-Sources-Rust.

Both tarballs are produced by packaging/create-tarballs.sh, which exports the
sources from Git and runs packaging/vendor.sh. Because vendoring refreshes the
lock files and the build uses --locked, that script refuses to create the
tarballs while the lock files in the working tree differ from the ones it would
export: commit them first. For the same reason the lock files are not listed in
debian/source/options: a difference has to show up in the Debian diff instead of
being dropped from the source package.

That script leaves the vendored dependencies in vendor/, so dpkg-buildpackage in
the working tree resolves them from there and stays offline as well.

debian/watch only serves to detect new upstream releases. The tarballs GitHub
publishes cannot be used directly, since they lack the vendor component.

Updating the packaging for a new upstream release
------------------------------------------------

 1. ./packaging/create-tarballs.sh, and commit the refreshed lock files if it
    asks for it. `debian/rules vendor-tarball` is an alias for it.
 2. Update XS-Vendored-Sources-Rust in debian/control from
    `debian/rules vendored-sources`. A build with the Ubuntu dh-cargo installed
    fails until the field matches the vendored tree.
 3. Check the licensing of the bundled crates, which
    ./packaging/vendor-licenses.sh reports, against the stanzas covering
    vendor/* in debian/copyright. Those stanzas summarise the vendored tree;
    ./packaging/vendor-licenses.sh -f dep5 generates the per-crate stanzas that
    replace them for an upload to the archive.
 4. The SONAME of the shared library is derived from the major version of the
    crate by lib/build.rs. When it changes, rename the libintel-crashlog1
    binary package accordingly.

The UEFI application
--------------------

The intel-crashlog-efi package is only built when the pkg.intel-crashlog.efi
build profile is enabled, on amd64. It needs a rustup managed toolchain for the
x86_64-unknown-uefi target, which the Rust toolchain packaged in Debian does not
provide, and installing it requires network access. It is therefore not suitable
for an official archive build.

